---

## Context

Sync is the host's terminal-typed command for propagating
`.aide/config/brain.aide` into the host's `.mcp.json`. It is the only
mutation surface for `.mcp.json`'s brain entry in the entire package:
no other code path — not the MCP server, not `aide_info`, not
`aide_init`, not `aide_upgrade`, not the cold-start CLI's brain
behavior (the cold-start CLI no longer touches the brain at all) —
writes `mcpServers["brain"]`. Every other surface either calls into
the same shared write helper sync uses, or routes the user to type
sync in their terminal.

The verb sits at a specific point in the lifecycle. After the user
runs `/aide:brain config` for the first time, that command's prose —
sourced from the integration's `config` body section in `brain.aide`
via `aide_brain({ kind: "config" })` — drives the agent through filling
the null slots in `mcpServerConfig.args` (e.g. for the bundled Obsidian
default, prompting for and writing the absolute brain root path into
`args[3]`) and ends with a sync invocation that propagates the now
fully-wired config into `.mcp.json`. After steady-state is reached,
sync runs again any time the user hand-edits `brain.aide` — to retarget
their brain at a different root, to upgrade to a new launcher version,
to swap integrations entirely. The user types it deliberately, watches
the output scroll past, and sees what changed.

The architectural reason is the visible-command-boundary invariant.
The host's `.mcp.json` is a load-bearing config file the user often
hand-edits — to point a brain at a temporary debug location, to
disable an MCP server while reproducing a bug, to add a personal MCP
integration. Mutations from inside an agent session are invisible from
the user's perspective; the user wakes up the next day to find their
`.mcp.json` changed by something they didn't type. Sync fixes this by
being the exclusive mutation path: every change to the brain entry is
a command the user typed, with output the user saw scroll past.

`brain.aide`'s `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]`. A
YAML null at any index is the explicit unwired-slot signal — it means
"the user has not yet supplied the value this slot needs." A launch
command containing null is not a launch command at all, so sync's
byte-faithful propagation contract terminates at the boundary: a
fully-wired config writes through; a null-bearing config halts and
routes the user to `/aide:brain config`. Sync sits at exactly the
seam where the unwired-slot signal must escape into the user's
attention rather than be laundered into a string in the launch
command.

## Strategy

**Byte-faithful propagation, scoped to `mcpServerConfig`.** Sync's
job is to take the post-interpolation `mcpServerConfig` returned by
`parseBrainAide` and write `{ command, args }` into
`mcpServers["brain"]` of the host's `.mcp.json`, preserving every
other key in the file verbatim. The parser owns the YAML; the
interpolator owns the `${...}` substitution surface in args; sync
owns the JSON merge and write. No re-reading args to infer slot
semantics, no per-arg validation, no re-parsing the prose body —
the entire transformation between disk and `.mcp.json` is a single
copy-out of one frontmatter object into one JSON key. The package
elsewhere swears off introspecting which arg index means what; sync
honors that by treating the post-interpolation args as an opaque
array of strings (or, in the failure case below, an array containing
nulls).

**Null-refusal precondition at the boundary, not transformation.**
If any element of `mcpServerConfig.args` is null at sync time, sync
exits non-zero, emits a stderr line that names the offending
index(es) explicitly (e.g. "args[3] is null — run /aide:brain config
to fill it"), routes the user to `/aide:brain config`, and does not
touch `.mcp.json`. This is a precondition check on the inbound
config, not a transformation of it. Sync does not coerce null to
empty string, drop the null entry from the args array, substitute
any sentinel string, or otherwise convert the structural unwired-slot
signal into a string in the launch command. The args array written
to `.mcp.json` is either the full post-interpolation array
byte-for-byte (every slot a string) or no write happens at all.

The reasoning is recorded in the parent spec's post-mortem on the
retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal sentinel: under that prior design, the
placeholder propagated verbatim into both `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json`,
the drift detector returned `ok` because the bytes agreed
byte-for-byte, the orchestrator never routed to `/aide:brain config`,
and the brain MCP server failed to launch silently — the user
discovered the broken state only after the launcher silently failed.
Any transformation at sync time — null-to-empty-string coercion,
null-entry dropping, sentinel substitution — reproduces that bug
class in different clothing: the unwired-slot signal would once
again be a string written into the launch command, the drift check
would once again return `ok` against a byte-faithful but unwireable
config, and the user would once again find out only when the launcher
fails. The structural fix at the parser layer is null-detection; the
structural fix at the sync layer is null-refusal. The two layers
together close the laundering hole.

**The stderr message names the index explicitly and routes to
`/aide:brain config`.** Sync has no agent surface and no
AskUserQuestion equivalent; the message is the user's only
diagnostic. Naming the offending index by number ("args[3]")
matches the per-integration `config` body section's vocabulary
(which names which indexes mean what for that integration), so the
user reading the message can cross-reference it directly against
the prose that documents what `args[3]` is supposed to be. When
multiple indexes are null, the message names all of them in one
line. The terminating sentence is fixed: "Run `/aide:brain config`
to fill the unwired slot(s)." That phrasing matches the orchestrator
routing the boot reporter uses for the `no-mcp-entry` brain status
state, so the user sees the same recovery path from both surfaces.

**Idempotency is byte-comparison-based, not state-machine-based.**
Sync computes the expected entry, reads the existing `.mcp.json`,
and if the existing `mcpServers.brain` is structurally equal to the
expected entry, sync writes nothing and reports "no changes."
Otherwise it writes. This matches how the cold-start CLI's existing
JSON-merge logic already behaves and matches the structural-equality
comparison `buildBrainState` uses for drift detection — same
operation on the same shape, kept consistent so the boot reporter's
`mcp-drift` state and sync's "no changes" path agree on what counts
as drift.

**Failure messages are short, name the file, name the action.**
Sync runs in plain terminal context; the user is reading the output
directly, with no agent to translate. Each error message names the
file path that's broken and what the user should do. The wording is
fixed (the implementor uses these strings):
- Missing brain.aide: "`.aide/config/brain.aide` not found. Run
  `/aide:brain config` to scaffold and wire it."
- Malformed frontmatter: "`.aide/config/brain.aide` frontmatter is
  malformed: `<parser reason verbatim>`. Fix the YAML and re-run
  sync."
- Malformed body markers: "`.aide/config/brain.aide` body is
  malformed: `<parser reason verbatim>`. Fix the markers and re-run
  sync."
- Null-bearing args: "`.aide/config/brain.aide` has unwired slots:
  `args[<index>]` is null. Run `/aide:brain config` to fill the
  unwired slot(s)."
- Malformed `.mcp.json`: "`.mcp.json` exists but contains invalid
  JSON. Fix the syntax error and re-run sync."

When the parser's reason names a deprecated field (`connector`,
`rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools`) or a deprecated marker pair
(pre-pivot heading-based bodies, three-section marker schemas, or
four-section schemas using the old `aide-prose-*`/`aide-playbook-*`/
`aide-research-*` markers), sync passes the parser's reason through
verbatim — the user reads exactly which stale fields or markers to
remove. Sync does not paraphrase or wrap; the parser owns the
schema and its rejection messages are the user's migration guide.

**Sync exits zero on success, non-zero on every other path.** The
exit code contract:
- 0 on successful write (with or without changes — "already in sync"
  is success).
- 1 on missing brain.aide, malformed brain.aide (frontmatter or
  body), null-bearing args, malformed `.mcp.json`, or write
  failure.
- 2 on `--help` or invalid argv (matches cold-start CLI
  conventions).

The null-refusal path uses exit code 1 alongside the other
brain.aide failure modes, because from the user's perspective it's
the same action class: the source file isn't ready, fix it and
re-run. The orchestrator flow that catches the boot reporter's
`no-mcp-entry` state and routes to `/aide:brain config` covers the
same recovery path; sync's job at the terminal is to surface the
problem, not to differentiate among kinds of unreadiness.

**Sync is a thin CLI wrapper over `parseBrainAide` + a shared
read/merge/write helper.** No business logic lives in this module;
every step is a delegation. The CLI's single responsibility is
sequencing the steps and emitting human-friendly terminal output.
The read/merge/write helper is shared with the cold-start CLI's
methodology-layer install (which writes the `aide` MCP entry, never
the `brain` entry). Both verbs go through the same JSON-merge
helper with the same key-preservation semantics — only the key and
the entry differ. The CLI is `node` script-style, not
Commander/Yargs, mirroring the cold-start CLI's pattern; the only
argv handling sync needs is `--help`. Paths are conventional:
`<cwd>/.aide/config/brain.aide` for the source,
`<cwd>/.mcp.json` for the destination. No override flags.

## Good examples

**A user runs sync on a fully-wired brain.aide for the first time.**
The user has just finished `/aide:brain config`, which scaffolded
`.aide/config/brain.aide` with `mcpServerConfig.args[3]` filled in to
their absolute brain root path and ran sync as the final step of the
flow. (Or: the user wired the slots by hand and is running sync
directly.) The terminal output:

```
Read .aide/config/brain.aide
Wrote brain MCP entry into .mcp.json
  command: npx
  args: ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "--rootDir", "D:/notes/my-brain"]
Done.
```

`.mcp.json`'s `mcpServers.brain` now carries the entry; every other
key (the user's `aide` entry, their `linear` MCP, their personal
custom MCP) is byte-identical to before. Exit code 0.

**The user runs sync immediately again to test idempotency.** The
output:

```
Read .aide/config/brain.aide
.mcp.json already in sync — no changes.
```

`.mcp.json`'s mtime is unchanged. Exit code 0.

**The user retargets their brain.** They open
`.aide/config/brain.aide`, edit `mcpServerConfig.args[3]` from
`"D:/notes/old-brain"` to `"D:/notes/new-brain"`, save, and run sync:

```
Read .aide/config/brain.aide
Wrote brain MCP entry into .mcp.json
  command: npx
  args: ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "--rootDir", "D:/notes/new-brain"]
Done.
```

`mcpServers.brain.args[3]` is the new path; every other key in
`.mcp.json` is preserved. Exit code 0.

**Sync hard-fails on a null-bearing args array.** The user has a
brain.aide whose `mcpServerConfig.args[3]` is YAML null (a bare `-`
on its own line in the args sequence) — perhaps because they
deleted the path to retarget but forgot to fill in the new value
before saving, or because they ran `aide_init({ category: "brain" })`
which seeded the template with a null at that slot and they ran
sync before completing `/aide:brain config`. They run sync. stderr:

```
Error: .aide/config/brain.aide has unwired slots: args[3] is null.
Run `/aide:brain config` to fill the unwired slot(s).
```

Exit code 1. `.mcp.json` is untouched — every key, including any
prior `mcpServers.brain` entry from a previous successful sync,
remains exactly as it was.

**Sync aborts on a brain.aide that still carries deprecated
fields.** The user upgraded the package but never migrated their
old `brain.aide`, which still has `rootPath`, `entryFile`, and
`tools` lines from the previous schema. They run sync. stderr:

```
Error: .aide/config/brain.aide frontmatter is malformed: deprecated
fields present: rootPath, entryFile, tools. Remove them and re-run
sync — the path now lives inline in mcpServerConfig.args.
```

Exit code 1. `.mcp.json` is untouched. The user removes the stale
fields, re-runs sync, and gets a clean write.

## Bad examples

**Sync silently coerces a null arg to an empty string.** Sync
detects `mcpServerConfig.args[3]` is null, replaces it with `""`,
and writes `mcpServers.brain.args = ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault",
"--rootDir", ""]` into `.mcp.json`. The launch command shape is
preserved on disk; the drift detector returns `ok` because
`brain.aide`'s parsed args (with null in the empty-string
position after sync's own coercion) match `.mcp.json`'s args
byte-for-byte; the orchestrator never routes to `/aide:brain
config`; the brain MCP server fails to launch silently with an
empty `--rootDir`. This reproduces the retired `<BRAIN_PATH>`
bug class in different clothing — the unwired-slot signal got
laundered into a string in the launch command, and the drift
check passed against a byte-faithful but unwireable config.

**Sync drops the null entry from the args array.** Sync detects
`mcpServerConfig.args[3]` is null, splices it out, and writes
`args = ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "--rootDir"]` into
`.mcp.json`. The launch command is now `npx -y @bitbonsai/mcpvault
--rootDir` with no value following the flag — an unstartable
launcher invocation. Same regression class: the null was
transformed into a string-shaped artifact in the launch command
instead of refused at the boundary.

**Sync substitutes a literal sentinel string for null.** Sync
replaces null at `args[3]` with `"<BRAIN_PATH>"`, `"TODO"`,
`"null"` (the string), `"<UNWIRED>"`, or any other in-band marker
the package or some convention "recognizes" as empty. The retired
`<BRAIN_PATH>` literal-sentinel design is the canonical example
of this regression and it must not return under any new name. YAML
null is the unwired-slot signal; the package never converts it
into a string under any pretense.

**Sync auto-runs on MCP server boot.** The package "helpfully"
calls sync's logic from `aide_info` so the boot reporter reflects
the latest `brain.aide` automatically. The user wakes up to find
their hand-edit of `.mcp.json` (a temporary debug brain path)
silently reverted. Worse: the user trusted that `.mcp.json` was
the source of truth for what brain was actually wired this
session, so the divergence between the boot reporter's view and
the actual MCP server's launcher (which still has the old config
in this session because it loaded at startup) makes diagnostic
information lie. Sync runs ONLY when the user types `npx aidemd-mcp
sync`. No exceptions — not from `aide_info`, not from `aide_init`,
not from `aide_upgrade`, not from the cold-start CLI, not from
any future helper that "just wants to make sure the brain is
wired right."

**Sync auto-runs from cli/init.** The cold-start CLI calls into
sync's logic at the end of `npx aidemd-mcp init` so the user
"doesn't have to remember." This violates two parent-spec
invariants simultaneously: cli/init must be brain-agnostic
(never write `.mcp.json`'s brain entry, never call
`provisionBrain`, never ask for a brain path), and sync must
require deliberate user invocation of the `npx aidemd-mcp sync`
command. Brain wiring is fully delegated to `/aide:brain config`
on the first `/aide` run after install; sync runs only when the
user types its name.

**Sync prompts the user when brain.aide is missing.**

```
Error: .aide/config/brain.aide not found. Where is your brain root? [enter path]
```

Sync has no agent surface and no AskUserQuestion equivalent;
treating the verb as interactive blurs the boundary between the
agent-mediated `/aide:brain config` flow and the user-typed-command
flow. The brain.aide on disk is the complete input set; if it's
missing or any slot is null, the user runs `/aide:brain config`
(which has the agent surface to interview) and re-runs sync.
Missing inputs are errors with exit code 1, not prompts.

**Sync interpolates the prose body.** Sync runs `${...}`
substitution across the prose body before storing it somewhere
or reading it for some derived purpose. The user's prose body
documenting `${variable}` placeholders for their knowledge store
is mangled. Sync only ever interpolates `mcpServerConfig.args`
via `interpolateArgs` (against any frontmatter field name the
user references — today only `${name}` is resolvable); the prose
body is for the brain tool to read, not for sync to touch.

**Sync overwrites the user's other MCP keys.** Sync reads
`.mcp.json`, computes a new object from `brain.aide`, and writes
`{ mcpServers: { brain: <entry> } }` — clobbering every other
server the user had configured. Sync must read the existing
config, merge the brain entry into it, and preserve all other keys
verbatim.

**Sync auto-creates a brain.aide when it's missing.** Sync detects
brain.aide is missing and writes a default Obsidian-shaped one
with a launcher command derived from a sibling-folder hint, then
continues to write `.mcp.json`. The user runs `npx aidemd-mcp sync`
after a fresh checkout and finds the package has scaffolded an
entire brain config they never asked for. Brain.aide creation is
`aide_init({ category: "brain" })`'s job (driven by `/aide:brain
config`); sync only propagates an existing brain.aide, never
creates one. This is the same regression class as `aide_upgrade`
mutating files under `.aide/config/` — sync may read that
directory, never write to it.

**Sync introspects args to validate a path.** Sync extracts
`mcpServerConfig.args[3]` (where the bundled Obsidian default's
`config` section names the brain root path), stats it, and
aborts with `invalid-path` if the directory doesn't exist. Path
validation is the launcher's job at MCP server startup; the
package does not second-guess it. The brain status state set is
exactly `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift` —
there is no `invalid-path` state, and re-introducing one through
sync is the same regression class as a `rootPath` field. Per-arg
slot semantics live in the user-owned `config` body section of
`brain.aide`, never in this CLI's code path.

**Sync auto-resolves drift by reading `.mcp.json` and writing
back to `brain.aide`.** Sync detects `mcpServers.brain` and
`brain.aide` disagree, decides `.mcp.json` is "more recent," and
patches `brain.aide` to match. Drift always resolves in one
direction: brain.aide → .mcp.json. The reverse direction would
let `.mcp.json` (which the user may hand-edit for debugging)
silently overwrite the user's authoritative config — exactly the
failure mode the parent spec's "auto-sync overwriting hand
edits" undesired outcome calls out. Same regression class as
`aide_upgrade` mutating `.aide/config/` files.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` -- parent spec's outcomes naming
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` as the canonical brain config path,
  the two-field schema (`name` + `mcpServerConfig`), the
  `(string | null)[]` typing of `mcpServerConfig.args` with YAML
  null as the unwired-slot signal, the brain status state set
  (`ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`), the
  no-introspection-of-arg-slots invariant, and the
  visible-command-boundary contract that names sync as the only
  `.mcp.json` brain-entry mutation surface; also the post-mortem
  on the retired `<BRAIN_PATH>` literal sentinel where placeholder
  bytes agreed in both files, the drift detector returned `ok`,
  and the launcher failed silently — the source of the
  null-refusal precondition design.
- `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` -- parser whose `parseBrainAide`
  and `interpolateArgs` exports this CLI consumes; defines the
  `(string | null)[]` typing of `mcpServerConfig.args` preserved
  verbatim through the parse pipeline (YAML null in args produces
  null in the parsed structure, never an empty string or sentinel)
  and the schema-rejection contract for deprecated fields and
  marker pairs that sync surfaces verbatim.
- `src/cli/init/.aide` -- cold-start CLI's spec, which establishes
  the brain-agnostic install contract (cli/init never writes
  brain.aide, never writes `mcpServers["brain"]`, never calls
  `provisionBrain`) and the read/merge/write JSON helper pattern
  that sync shares for `.mcp.json` mutation with the same
  key-preservation semantics.
- `src/service/buildBrainState/.aide` (if present) -- boot
  reporter's drift detection, whose structural-equality
  comparison on `mcpServerConfig` shape sync mirrors for
  idempotency, and whose four-state vocabulary (`ok`,
  `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift`) maps null-bearing
  args to `no-mcp-entry` — the same recovery path sync's
  null-refusal stderr message routes the user toward.
